## Why MPL-2.0 at the root

cdktn-awscc is one of the cdktn-io "provider" family of packages (alongside cdktn-io/cdk-terrain
itself and cdktn-io/cdktn-provider-*), all of which are MPL-2.0. The package's runtime core —
the parser/model/emitter pipeline under tools/awscc2cdk/src/grouped/ and its unmodified
counterpart under tools/awscc2cdk/src/vendored/cdktn/ — is an adaptation of
cdktn-io/cdk-terrain's `@cdktn/provider-generator` (itself MPL-2.0, HashiCorp-originated), and the
generated/ tree it produces (1,494 resource bindings across 276 modules — the actual shipped
product) is a Terraform CDK provider binding in the same family as cdktn-provider-awscc. Root
MPL-2.0 keeps this package's license consistent with the rest of the fleet.

This is a free choice, not a legal requirement: MPL-2.0 §3.3 explicitly allows combining
MPL-covered files with differently-licensed files into a "Larger Work" distributed under terms of
your choosing, provided each MPL-covered *file* keeps its MPL notice and remains available under
MPL-2.0. Apache-2.0 §4 imposes no restriction on the license of a combined/larger work either,
provided attribution and modification notices are preserved. Both conditions are met here (see
below) — so the choice of MPL-2.0 as the root/package license was cdktn-io's, made for fleet
consistency with the org's other provider packages, not because either upstream license compelled
it.

**Flag for human review:** `package.json` previously declared `"license": "Apache-2.0"`; this pass
changed it to `"MPL-2.0"` to match the reasoning above. That is a user-facing change (it affects
what license text package registries display) and is worth a maintainer sign-off before release,
even though nothing here required Apache-2.0 — the package's own prior declaration was itself just
a placeholder from early scaffolding, not a considered decision either way.

